WEBVTT

00:00:00.148 --> 00:00:05.817
Now we move from reading data to changing stored rows, so our safety habits become more important.

00:00:05.817 --> 00:00:09.107
Before an UPDATE or DELETE, preview the target rows with SELECT.

00:00:09.107 --> 00:00:11.772
Use the same WHERE condition you intend to use in the write statement.

00:00:11.772 --> 00:00:19.797
Whenever possible, identify rows with a stable primary key or unique key rather than a name or other value

00:00:19.797 --> 00:00:20.192
that may repeat.

00:00:20.192 --> 00:00:25.347
A transaction can provide another layer of protection because you can inspect a change before deciding whether

00:00:25.347 --> 00:00:26.792
to keep it.

00:00:26.792 --> 00:00:31.217
RETURNING is also very useful in PostgreSQL because it immediately shows the rows that were inserted, updated,

00:00:31.217 --> 00:00:33.857
or deleted.

00:00:33.857 --> 00:00:37.852
The most important warning is simple: an UPDATE or DELETE without an appropriate WHERE clause can affect every

00:00:37.852 --> 00:00:42.707
row in the table. Writes deserve a slower workflow than reads.

00:00:42.707 --> 00:00:49.297
Before UPDATE or DELETE, run the intended WHERE condition as a SELECT and inspect the affected rows.

00:00:49.297 --> 00:00:52.522
In production, use transactions when appropriate so you can verify the change before committing.

00:00:52.522 --> 00:00:57.760
The absence of a syntax error does not mean the correct records were modified.
